  "preamble": "The voiceless dental fricative [θ] becomes the postalveolar [ʃ] through palatalization, usually next to high front vowels or glides, or through mergers in which the interdental joins a sibilant series. Proto-Semitic *θ merged with *š in Canaanite (Hebrew šéleḡ 'snow', šôr 'ox') and in Akkadian (šalgum, šūrum), while Arabic kept [θ] and Aramaic turned it into t. Some Upper Tiyare dialects of North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic changed θ to š after a contracted *ay and in the ending *-iθa. In Mapudungun, replacing /θ/ with [ʃ] is part of an expressive (diminutive, endearing) palatalization.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Palatalization, Expressive Palatalization, Merger",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Biblical Hebrew",
      "languageFamily": "Canaanite",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *θalāθ- 'three'",
          "to": "Biblical Hebrew šālōš (שָׁלֹשׁ)",
          "note": "Regular merger of *θ with *š in Canaanite (Phoenician šlš too); Arabic keeps [θ] in ثلاث, and Akkadian has šalāšum."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *ṯalg- 'snow'",
          "to": "שֶׁלֶג šéleḡ 'snow'"
        },
